    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's helpful for the libertarian side. Now, to keep the essay balanced, can you do the same for the social democratic side? I don't want to just summarize them as 'people who want more services.' I need the actual philosophical depth—like how they use the concept of distributive justice or how they view the social contract. What are the most sophisticated, robust arguments they use to counter the idea of absolute self-ownership? I want to make sure their position is represented with the same level of intellectual rigor as the libertarian one.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Social democrats often rely on a more nuanced understanding of distributive justice, arguing that individual rights and freedoms can be subject to reasonable limits when they disproportionately benefit the few at the expense of the many. Distributive justice, as developed by philosophers like John Rawls, considers the impact of social and economic structures on the well-being of all members of society.\n\nRegarding the social contract, social democrats may invoke Rawls' \"Acquisition Principle,\" which suggests that individuals should stick to the social contract only if they recognize that everyone has recently entered into it with an equal \"initial position\" where their reasonable expectations of well-being and justice are bilateralized, i.e., each person's own talents and abilities being supposed equal under these terms.",
